{"title":"2004 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 11","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/9/1697819f92b5937788c126eb111a99f6102cbe08.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-2, 4, 6, 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"74\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> are rearranged according to these rules:\n</p>\n<pre>        1. The largest isn't first, but it is in one of the first three places. \n        2. The smallest isn't last, but it is in one of the last three places. \n        3. The median isn't first or last.\n</pre>\n<p>What is the average of the first and last numbers?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/2/0a23fc06b89b7cc5977ec414d39f5b3d40209c49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 3.5 \\qquad \\textbf{(B)}\\ 5 \\qquad \\textbf{(C)}\\ 6.5 \\qquad \\textbf{(D)}\\ 7.5 \\qquad \\textbf{(E)}\\ 8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"391\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>From rule 1, the largest number, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, can be second or third. From rule 2, because there are five places, the smallest number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/a/62a76677add1d246708c86edfc7c4b8a40184914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" /> can either be third or fourth. The median, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> can be second, third, or fourth. Because we know the middle three numbers, the first and last numbers are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, disregarding their order. Their average is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/7/06731c47a1179472844b0e7459b8f2a555046da3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(4+9)/2 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\ 6.5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"166\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_8\" title=\"2004 AMC 8\">2004 AMC 8</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_8_Problems\" title=\"